I have a diagnosed broken circadian rhythm and cortisol issues, so sleep has never come easily for me — I'm not someone with the occasional restless night; I'm someone who has had to build an entire routine around a body that doesn't want to cooperate. This is the melatonin that's actually earned a permanent place in that routine, and the reason has as much to do with the dose as anything else.

Most melatonin sold on store shelves comes in 5mg or 10mg tablets. That sounds like it should be the stronger, better-working option. It isn't. The research on melatonin dosing consistently points to a much narrower effective range — roughly 0.3 to 1 milligram — that performs as well as or better than higher doses, with less next-day grogginess. This product's 1mg dose sits right at the top of that range instead of well above it, and that's the main reason I chose it over the mega-dose bottles that dominate most drugstore shelves.

It's a fast-dissolve, strawberry-flavored tablet— no water required. I take it about 30 minutes before I actually want to be asleep, the same way the label recommends, and let it dissolve in my mouth. Paired with magnesium glycinate, it's been genuinely instrumental in helping me fall asleep more quickly and reliably get at least 4 hours of sleep, even on nights where my body's internal clock is actively working against me.

I want to be straightforward about what that does and doesn't prove. This isn't a cure for a broken circadian rhythm or cortisol dysregulation — nothing sold over the counter is — and 4 hours is still short of what a healthy adult needs. What I can say honestly is that this specific product, at this specific dose, has made a real, noticeable difference in how quickly I fall asleep and how much sleep I actually get, given a genuinely difficult starting point. Natrol's own marketing claims it starts working in as little as 15 minutes; I can't independently verify that specific timing, but it dissolves quickly and doesn't leave a bad aftertaste, which is more than I can say for some other fast-dissolve supplements I've tried.

If you're currently taking a 5mg or 10mg melatonin and not getting the results you want, it may genuinely be worth trying a lower dose like this one before assuming melatonin just doesn't work for you. The research suggests less is often more here, and my own experience backs that up.
